From: Rens Troost <rens@lorax.imsi.com>
Subject: NET: commercial use

Brian Said:

	since nearly all commercial users have a commercial internet provider,
	who has a connection to the Commercial Internet Exchange (CIX). 

Would that it were true! Unfortunately, ANS (who operates NSFnet) is
violently opposed to the CIX. Recently the CIX has been doing
agreesive filtering of non-AUP traffic onto the NSFnet, and
coincidentally onto ANSnet. Net result: non AUP customers of PSI,
ALTERnet, JVNC, etc. cannot get to ANS commercial sites. Ugh.

In the long run, I reckon ANS will buckle and join the CIX. This is
not happening soon, and probably would require the loss of their
NSFnet contract.

This is all how I understand it. If someone has better information,
please let me know.

	You can always use 'traceroute' to make sure your packets are going to
	stay on commercial networks.

Routing tables subject to change without notice. :)

-Rens

